// prism_text -- Prism's OWN text measurement/wrap/truncate/pad layer. Milestone MB.10.
//
// ── WHY THIS FILE EXISTS ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// Every Prism component renders text, and until now nothing could wrap, truncate, or measure it.
// `backend/ansi/prism_render_ansi.nova` already takes a `cols` parameter (`prism_ansi_render_width`)
// it cannot honour -- a label wider than the terminal just overflows. A `grid`/table column has no
// way to fit a long value into a fixed width. This file is that missing layer.
//
// ── WHY PRISM OWNS THIS INSTEAD OF DEFERRING TO A BROWSER/OS TEXT-SHAPING API ─────────────────
// NOVA_DESIGN/PRISM_STATUS.md F3 records that "inherit the browser's shaping via fillText into a
// glyph atlas" is IMPOSSIBLE: `TextMetrics` exposes 12 whole-string properties and ZERO per-glyph
// data, and there is no cross-browser way to know which characters in a string correspond to which
// rendered glyphs. Flutter's answer was to bundle HarfBuzz+ICU (a 1-2 MB brotli payload) precisely
// because it could not lean on the browser's shaper either. Prism's answer, for the ANSI/table use
// case this milestone targets, is smaller and honest: a monospace CELL model (every codepoint is 0,
// 1, or 2 terminal columns wide, per Unicode's East Asian Width property), not full glyph shaping.
// That is the right model for a terminal backend and for fixed-width table columns; it is NOT a
// claim to solve proportional-font text shaping for a future GPU/DOM backend, which is a materially
// different and much larger problem left to whichever milestone builds that backend.
//
// ── PROBE: DOES NOVA'S `len()` COUNT BYTES OR CHARACTERS? ─────────────────────────────────────
// Answer, established directly (not assumed) from THREE independent sources before writing a
// single line of the width logic below:
//   1. `nova_runtime.c`'s `nova_rt_len`/`nova_rt_str_len` return `strlen(s)` (or a cached fat-string
//      length that is ITSELF the byte count written at construction) -- there is no UTF-8 decoding
//      anywhere in that path.
//   2. `nova_rt_chr(n)` masks `n & 0xFF` into a single byte, and `nova_rt_ord(s)` returns
//      `(unsigned char)s[0]` -- the first BYTE, not a decoded codepoint. Neither function can
//      produce or consume a multi-byte character; they are byte-level primitives.
//   3. `std/text/grapheme.nova` (gr_split/gr_len) independently confirms this by NEEDING its own
//      hand-rolled UTF-8 decoder over `str_to_bytes`/`bytes_get` specifically BECAUSE `len()` and
//      `ord()`/`chr()` are byte-oriented -- if `len()` already counted characters, that decoder
//      would be pointless work its own author would not have done.
// CONCLUSION, stated plainly: **`len()` counts BYTES, not characters.** A 2-byte UTF-8 character
// (e.g. "e" + combining accent's precomposed form "é") has `len() == 2` but is ONE character
// occupying ONE terminal cell; a 3-byte CJK character has `len() == 3` but occupies TWO terminal
// cells. Neither number may be used as a proxy for the other anywhere in this file -- every width
// computation below decodes real UTF-8 codepoints first (mirroring grapheme.nova's proven
// `bytes_get`-based scanning, re-implemented here rather than imported: `_gr_char_len`/
// `_gr_decode_cp` are module-PRIVATE in grapheme.nova, so they are not even reachable from another
// file, and this module's job per F3 above is to own its OWN text measurement, not borrow another
// module's private internals).
//
// ── WHAT ALREADY EXISTS IN std/, AND WHY IT IS NOT REUSED HERE (CLAUDE.md's reuse rule) ────────
// Searched by CONCEPT before writing anything: std/text/{wcwidth,wrap,truncate,pad,grapheme,
// graphemex}.nova and std/textlayout/{wordwrap,truncate_text,textpad,textcenter,reflow,
// linebreak}.nova all exist and were read in full. Findings:
//   - `std/text/wcwidth.nova`'s `ww_char_width(cp)` is a correct PER-CODEPOINT classifier (given a
//     real codepoint, its East-Asian-Width verdict is right) -- but `ww_string_width(s)` DOES NOT
//     decode UTF-8: it walks `s` one BYTE at a time and charges every byte > 127 as width 1. For a
//     3-byte CJK character that reports width 3 (one per byte), not the correct 2. This is exactly
//     the bug this milestone's brief warns against ("a multi-byte character must not count as 2+
//     cells") -- verified by tracing the code, not assumed, and it disqualifies `ww_string_width`
//     for this file's `prism_text_width`. (`ww_char_width` alone, if fed a real decoded codepoint,
//     would have been reusable; NOVA's file-local-only import surface plus the trivial size of a
//     correct classifier made re-deriving it not worth a cross-module round-trip for one function.)
//   - `std/text/wrap.nova`, `std/text/truncate.nova`, `std/text/pad.nova` all measure in `len()`,
//     i.e. BYTES -- correct for ASCII, silently wrong for any accented or CJK content (the exact
//     failure mode this milestone exists to close). Not reusable for the same reason as above.
//   - `std/text/grapheme.nova` is the one genuinely reusable IDEA: its byte-scanning algorithm
//     (`str_to_bytes`/`bytes_get`, validate continuation bytes, decode the codepoint) is the
//     correct pattern and is re-implemented here (see probe note above for why "re-implemented"
//     rather than "imported"). Grapheme CLUSTERS (combining marks glued to a base, ZWJ sequences)
//     are a strictly finer-grained concept than terminal CELL WIDTH and are not needed here: this
//     file only needs "how many columns does this codepoint occupy", which combining marks answer
//     as 0 regardless of cluster membership.
//   - `std/textlayout/*` (6 more modules) were opened and are same-shape variations on the above
//     (byte-length wrap/truncate/pad/reflow) -- same disqualification, not reproduced in detail here
//     to avoid repeating the same finding six times.
// CLAUDE.md also forbids editing anything under std/ for this milestone, so even the one reusable
// idea (grapheme.nova's scanning pattern) could only be reproduced, never imported and fixed in
// place.
//
// ── DESIGN DECISION: THE ELLIPSIS IS ASCII "...", NOT THE UNICODE "…" GLYPH ────────────────────
// Exactly the same reasoning `backend/ansi/prism_render_ansi.nova`'s `_ansi_glyph_char` already
// recorded for icons: this must be legible in a plain VT100 / CI log / Windows console with no font
// fallback, where a single-codepoint ellipsis can render as a tofu box or a double-width glyph
// depending on the font. Three ASCII periods are width-3, always render, and their width is
// self-evidently `len("...")` cells with no decoding needed at all.
//
// ── DESIGN DECISION: TRUNCATION CAN PAD BY ONE CELL TO HIT THE TARGET WIDTH EXACTLY ────────────
// The brief's hard requirement is "the result's measured width is exactly `width`, never
// `width + 1`" -- but a wide (2-cell) character makes "exactly" sometimes UNREACHABLE by content
// alone: if the remaining budget after reserving the ellipsis is an odd number of cells and every
// candidate character is 2 cells wide, the greediest safe prefix undershoots the budget by exactly
// 1 cell (taking one more character would overshoot it by 1 instead -- both illegal, since the
// requirement is "exactly", never `+1` and, symmetrically, never `-1` either). The only content-
// preserving fix is a single trailing pad space absorbing that leftover cell, so the width contract
// holds unconditionally rather than "usually, except for wide-character content" -- the latter
// would be exactly the kind of undefined-behaviour-for-CJK gap this milestone exists to close.
// Proven bounded: since every codepoint here is worth 0, 1, or 2 cells, the shortfall this can ever
// produce is 0 or 1 cell -- never more.
//
// ── DESIGN DECISION: THE WRAP ROUND-TRIP CONTRACT, STATED PRECISELY ────────────────────────────
// "Preserve every character" is asserted for text whose words are separated by exactly one space,
// with no leading/trailing whitespace and no run of multiple spaces -- i.e. `join(prism_text_wrap
// (s, w), " ") == s` whenever `s` needs no hard-break. This matches `std/text/wrap.nova`'s own
// documented scope (it also collapses whitespace runs), and is the same reason `_ptx_split_words`
// below drops empty tokens rather than preserving a run of spaces byte-for-byte: preserving both
// "arbitrary whitespace runs" AND "wrap re-flows text" simultaneously is a different, harder
// contract (Markdown-style whitespace-significant text) that nothing in this milestone's brief asks
// for. A word that gets hard-broken is, BY CONSTRUCTION, never split at a space (splitting only
// ever happens mid-word, at a codepoint boundary that is not a space), so the pieces of one
// hard-broken word concatenate back together with NO separator -- only distinct words are ever
// rejoined with a single space.
//
// PUBLIC API
//   prism_text_width(s)                     -> int             monospace terminal cells (0/1/2 per
//                                                                 codepoint; NOT `len()`, which is
//                                                                 bytes -- see probe note above)
//   prism_text_wrap(s, width)                -> Result<list>    greedy word-wrap, hard-breaks a
//                                                                 single overlong word; rejects
//                                                                 width < 1
//   prism_text_truncate(s, width)            -> Result<string>  cuts to EXACTLY `width` cells with
//                                                                 a trailing "..."; rejects a width
//                                                                 smaller than the ellipsis itself
//   prism_text_pad_left(s, width)            -> Result<string>  pad spaces on the LEFT (right-aligns
//                                                                 content); never truncates
//   prism_text_pad_right(s, width)           -> Result<string>  pad spaces on the RIGHT (left-aligns
//                                                                 content); never truncates
//   prism_text_pad_center(s, width)          -> Result<string>  pad both sides; an odd remainder
//                                                                 goes on the RIGHT (matches
//                                                                 std/text/pad.nova's `center`)
//   prism_text_label_wrapped(s, width)       -> Result<PrismNode>  a `stack` of one `label` per
//                                                                 wrapped line, so wrapping composes
//                                                                 with the existing HTML/ANSI
//                                                                 renderers with NO backend changes

import prism_node
import prism_arrange
import prism_ui_kit

// ── UTF-8 decoding (self-contained -- see header on why this is re-derived, not imported) ───────
// Same validated algorithm shape as std/text/grapheme.nova's private `_gr_char_len`/`_gr_decode_cp`:
// malformed or truncated sequences degrade to a single raw byte rather than over-reading or
// panicking, so decoding is always TOTAL.

fn _ptx_char_len(bts, i: int, n: int) -> int
    let b0 = bytes_get(bts, i)
    if b0 < 128
        return 1
    if b0 >= 192 and b0 < 224 and i + 1 < n
        let b1 = bytes_get(bts, i + 1)
        if b1 >= 128 and b1 < 192
            return 2
        return 1
    if b0 >= 224 and b0 < 240 and i + 2 < n
        let b1 = bytes_get(bts, i + 1)
        let b2 = bytes_get(bts, i + 2)
        if b1 >= 128 and b1 < 192 and b2 >= 128 and b2 < 192
            return 3
        return 1
    if b0 >= 240 and b0 < 248 and i + 3 < n
        let b1 = bytes_get(bts, i + 1)
        let b2 = bytes_get(bts, i + 2)
        let b3 = bytes_get(bts, i + 3)
        if b1 >= 128 and b1 < 192 and b2 >= 128 and b2 < 192 and b3 >= 128 and b3 < 192
            return 4
        return 1
    return 1

fn _ptx_decode_cp(bts, i: int, blen: int) -> int
    let b0 = bytes_get(bts, i)
    if blen == 1
        return b0
    if blen == 2
        let b1 = bytes_get(bts, i + 1)
        return ((b0 - 192) * 64) + (b1 - 128)
    if blen == 3
        let b1 = bytes_get(bts, i + 1)
        let b2 = bytes_get(bts, i + 2)
        return ((b0 - 224) * 4096) + ((b1 - 128) * 64) + (b2 - 128)
    let b1 = bytes_get(bts, i + 1)
    let b2 = bytes_get(bts, i + 2)
    let b3 = bytes_get(bts, i + 3)
    return ((b0 - 240) * 262144) + ((b1 - 128) * 4096) + ((b2 - 128) * 64) + (b3 - 128)

// ── Cell-width classification (Unicode UAX #11 "East Asian Width") ──────────────────────────────
// 0  control characters (cp < 32 or cp == 127) and zero-width marks -- combining diacriticals
//    (U+0300-U+036F) and the zero-width space/non-joiner/joiner (U+200B/U+200C/U+200D). DELIBERATE
//    DIVERGENCE from std/text/wcwidth.nova's `ww_char_width`, which reports -1 for controls: that
//    function serves a terminal-CURSOR model (a control character can MOVE the cursor, which is a
//    different question from "how many cells does this occupy"). This module only ever sums cell
//    counts for wrap/truncate/pad bookkeeping over text the ANSI backend has already sanitized of
//    control bytes (`prism_ansi_sanitize` strips them before display) -- charging 0 keeps every sum
//    here non-negative and total, with no tri-state handling needed at every call site.
// 2  East Asian WIDE and FULLWIDTH ranges (UAX #11 categories W and F): Hangul Jamo, CJK radicals/
//    punctuation, Hiragana/Katakana, CJK Unified Ideographs (+ Extension A), Hangul Syllables, CJK
//    Compatibility Ideographs, and the Fullwidth Forms block. These occupy TWO terminal columns.
// 1  everything else (ASCII, Latin-1 Supplement incl. precomposed accents like "e" -> U+00E9,
//    Cyrillic, Greek, Arabic, most punctuation/symbols) -- ONE terminal column.
fn _ptx_char_width(cp: int) -> int
    if cp < 32 or cp == 127
        return 0
    if cp >= 0x0300 and cp <= 0x036F
        return 0
    if cp == 0x200B or cp == 0x200C or cp == 0x200D
        return 0
    if cp >= 0x1100 and cp <= 0x115F
        return 2
    if cp >= 0x2E80 and cp <= 0x303E
        return 2
    if cp >= 0x3041 and cp <= 0x33FF
        return 2
    if cp >= 0x3400 and cp <= 0x4DBF
        return 2
    if cp >= 0x4E00 and cp <= 0x9FFF
        return 2
    if cp >= 0xAC00 and cp <= 0xD7A3
        return 2
    if cp >= 0xF900 and cp <= 0xFAFF
        return 2
    if cp >= 0xFF00 and cp <= 0xFF60
        return 2
    if cp >= 0xFFE0 and cp <= 0xFFE6
        return 2
    1

// Monospace cell width of a whole string: decode every codepoint (UTF-8 boundaries only -- never a
// raw byte split) and sum its classified width. NOT `len(s)`, which is a BYTE count (see the probe
// note in the file header) -- e.g. `len("e-acute precomposed")` charges 2 bytes for a 1-cell
// character, and `len("CJK char")` charges 3 bytes for a 2-cell character. Neither number is usable
// here; this is why this function exists at all.
fn prism_text_width(s: string) -> int
    if len(s) == 0
        return 0
    let bts = str_to_bytes(s)
    let n = bytes_len(bts)
    let acc_w = 0
    let i = 0
    while i < n
        let blen = _ptx_char_len(bts, i, n)
        let cp = _ptx_decode_cp(bts, i, blen)
        acc_w += _ptx_char_width(cp)
        i += blen
    acc_w

// ── Word splitting (byte-safe even over multi-byte content) ─────────────────────────────────────
// Splits on literal ASCII space (0x20) only, dropping empty tokens from consecutive spaces (matches
// std/text/wrap.nova's and std/text/truncate.nova's `_tr_split_words` convention -- see header on
// the round-trip contract this implies: leading/trailing/doubled whitespace is not preserved).
// Scanning one BYTE at a time (via `char_at`, not a UTF-8 decode) is still correct in the presence
// of multi-byte characters: 0x20 can never occur as a lead byte (0xC2-0xF4) or a continuation byte
// (0x80-0xBF) of a well-formed UTF-8 sequence, so a byte that compares equal to " " is UNAMBIGUOUSLY
// a real space, and every other byte (including every byte of a multi-byte character) is appended
// to the current word untouched, so multi-byte content inside a word is never corrupted.
fn _ptx_split_words(s: string) -> list
    let words = []
    let cur = ""
    let i = 0
    let n = len(s)
    while i < n
        let ch = char_at(s, i)
        if ch == " "
            if len(cur) > 0
                words.push(cur)
                cur = ""
        else
            cur = cur + ch
        i += 1
    if len(cur) > 0
        words.push(cur)
    words

// Breaks a single word (one whose OWN width exceeds `width`) into the fewest possible UTF-8-safe
// chunks, each at most `width` cells wide. Cuts only ever land on codepoint boundaries (the byte
// offset `i` always advances a whole `blen` at a time, so `word[start:i]` never bisects a multi-byte
// sequence). A codepoint whose OWN width exceeds `width` (e.g. a 2-cell CJK character requested into
// a width-1 budget) is still placed alone in its own chunk rather than dropped or split further --
// there is no narrower unit to cut it into, so total-ness (always terminates, always makes forward
// progress, never crashes) wins over a strict "never exceeds width" guarantee that is provably
// impossible to keep for this one input shape.
fn _ptx_hard_break(word: string, width: int) -> list
    let chunks = []
    let bts = str_to_bytes(word)
    let n = bytes_len(bts)
    let cur_start = 0
    let cur_w = 0
    let i = 0
    while i < n
        let blen = _ptx_char_len(bts, i, n)
        let cp = _ptx_decode_cp(bts, i, blen)
        let cw = _ptx_char_width(cp)
        if cur_w > 0 and cur_w + cw > width
            chunks.push(word[cur_start:i])
            cur_start = i
            cur_w = 0
        cur_w += cw
        i += blen
    if cur_start < n
        chunks.push(word[cur_start:n])
    chunks

// Greedy word-wrap, breaking on spaces, never mid-word unless a single word's OWN width exceeds
// `width` (then `_ptx_hard_break` splits it). Line-fit accounting uses `prism_text_width`
// (cell-width), not `len()` -- a table column full of CJK text must wrap at the right visual point,
// not the right byte count. See the header for the exact round-trip contract this satisfies.
fn prism_text_wrap(s: string, width: int) -> Result<list>
    if width < 1
        return err("prism_text_wrap: width must be >= 1, got " + str(width))
    if len(s) == 0
        return ok([])
    let words = _ptx_split_words(s)
    if len(words) == 0
        return ok([])
    let lines = []
    let cur = ""
    let cur_w = 0
    for word in words
        let ww = prism_text_width(word)
        if ww <= width
            if cur_w == 0
                cur = word
                cur_w = ww
            else if cur_w + 1 + ww <= width
                cur = cur + " " + word
                cur_w = cur_w + 1 + ww
            else
                lines.push(cur)
                cur = word
                cur_w = ww
        else
            // The word alone is wider than the line -- flush whatever is in progress first, then
            // lay the word's own hard-broken chunks down as full lines, keeping only its LAST
            // (possibly partial) chunk as the new in-progress line so a following short word can
            // still share that line if there's room.
            if cur_w > 0
                lines.push(cur)
                cur = ""
                cur_w = 0
            let chunks = _ptx_hard_break(word, width)
            let clen = len(chunks)
            let ci = 0
            while ci < clen - 1
                lines.push(chunks[ci])
                ci += 1
            cur = chunks[clen - 1]
            cur_w = prism_text_width(cur)
    if len(cur) > 0
        lines.push(cur)
    ok(lines)

// The ellipsis is ASCII "..." -- see header design decision. Its own cell width (3) is computed
// through `prism_text_width` rather than hardcoded so it stays self-consistent if this constant
// ever changes.
let PTX_ELLIPSIS = "..."

// Cuts `s` to EXACTLY `width` cells with a trailing "...", never `width + 1` and never `width - 1`
// either (see header design decision on the one-cell pad that makes "exactly" reachable even when
// the remaining budget can't be hit precisely by wide-character content alone). Rejects a width
// smaller than the ellipsis's own width -- there is no way to render "truncated, plus an ellipsis"
// in less space than the ellipsis itself needs, so this is checked unconditionally up front (not
// only when a given string would actually need truncating), keeping the contract independent of
// input content.
fn prism_text_truncate(s: string, width: int) -> Result<string>
    let ell_w = prism_text_width(PTX_ELLIPSIS)
    if width < ell_w
        return err("prism_text_truncate: width must be >= " + str(ell_w) + " (the ellipsis's own width), got " + str(width))
    if prism_text_width(s) <= width
        return ok(s)
    let budget = width - ell_w
    let bts = str_to_bytes(s)
    let n = bytes_len(bts)
    let i = 0
    let prefix_w = 0
    let scanning = true
    while scanning and i < n
        let blen = _ptx_char_len(bts, i, n)
        let cp = _ptx_decode_cp(bts, i, blen)
        let cw = _ptx_char_width(cp)
        if prefix_w + cw > budget
            scanning = false
        else
            prefix_w += cw
            i += blen
    let prefix = s[0:i]
    // Bounded 0 or 1: every codepoint here is worth 0, 1, or 2 cells, so the greedy prefix above can
    // undershoot `budget` by at most 1 (a 2-cell character that didn't fit into a 1-cell remainder).
    let deficit = budget - prefix_w
    ok(prefix + PTX_ELLIPSIS + _ptx_rep_space(deficit))

fn _ptx_rep_space(n: int) -> string
    let out = ""
    let i = 0
    while i < n
        out = out + " "
        i += 1
    out

// ── Padding/alignment (cell-width, not byte-length) ──────────────────────────────────────────────
// Mirrors std/text/pad.nova's ljust/rjust/center semantics (never truncates; a wider-than-target
// string is returned unchanged, since shrinking it is truncate's job, not pad's) but measured in
// terminal cells so multi-byte content pads correctly. Rejects a negative width outright; width 0
// is legal (it simply never needs padding).

fn prism_text_pad_left(s: string, width: int) -> Result<string>
    if width < 0
        return err("prism_text_pad_left: width must be >= 0, got " + str(width))
    let sw = prism_text_width(s)
    if sw >= width
        return ok(s)
    ok(_ptx_rep_space(width - sw) + s)

fn prism_text_pad_right(s: string, width: int) -> Result<string>
    if width < 0
        return err("prism_text_pad_right: width must be >= 0, got " + str(width))
    let sw = prism_text_width(s)
    if sw >= width
        return ok(s)
    ok(s + _ptx_rep_space(width - sw))

// Odd leftover padding goes on the RIGHT -- same tie-break std/text/pad.nova's `center` already
// established for this codebase; no reason to invent a different convention here.
fn prism_text_pad_center(s: string, width: int) -> Result<string>
    if width < 0
        return err("prism_text_pad_center: width must be >= 0, got " + str(width))
    let sw = prism_text_width(s)
    if sw >= width
        return ok(s)
    let total_pad = width - sw
    let left = total_pad / 2
    let right = total_pad - left
    ok(_ptx_rep_space(left) + s + _ptx_rep_space(right))

// ── Composition: a wrapped label is a stack of labels, not a backend feature ─────────────────────
// This is MB.10's real payoff: wrapping composes with the EXISTING renderers (HTML, ANSI) with zero
// backend changes, because a `stack` of `label`s is already in the closed 22-primitive vocabulary
// and both renderers already walk `stack` generically. Reuses `prism_arrange.prism_stack` (the
// vocabulary's own stack constructor) and `prism_ui_kit.prism_ui_labels` (the already-built
// strings-to-label-nodes-with-error-propagation helper, MB.2) rather than re-deriving either --
// exactly the reuse CLAUDE.md asks for, and the shortest correct path to this function.
fn prism_text_label_wrapped(s: string, width: int) -> Result<PrismNode>
    let wrapped = prism_text_wrap(s, width)
    if is_err(wrapped)
        return err(unwrap_err(wrapped))
    let lbls = prism_ui_kit.prism_ui_labels(unwrap(wrapped))
    if is_err(lbls)
        return err(unwrap_err(lbls))
    prism_arrange.prism_stack(unwrap(lbls))
